<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <ScenTitle>The Shark</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;B&gt;Original Concept By:&lt;/B&gt; Paul Bridge&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;B&gt;Date:&lt;/B&gt; Decembet 9th 1971 &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Location:&lt;/B&gt; Indian Ocean &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Playable Sides:&lt;/B&gt; India / Pakistan &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Duration:&lt;/B&gt; 1 Day &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;By late 1971 tensions between West Pakistan (today known as Pakistan) and East Pakistan (Bangladesh) were growing. An Indian intervention in Indian-influenced East Pakistan seemed increasingly likely, and on 21st November the Pakistan Navy deployed PNS Ghazi--an ex-US Navy Tench-class diesel-electric submarine--to the distant waters of the Bay of Bengal. Simultaneously the Pakistan Navy deployed PNS Hangor--a Daphné class diesel-electric submarine--to the coastal waters off the Indian Kathiawar coast. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;On the night of 2nd December 1971 a large formation of Indian Navy ships passed PNS Hangor at close range in waters off Bombay. The Indian task force included the cruiser INS Mysore, the flagship of the Indian fleet. Certain that this indicated a likely attack, the captain of PNS Hangor transmitted a warning of a possible attack by the Indian naval fleet on Pakistani forces stationed in Karachi. Indian naval intelligence intercepted these transmissions, and two ASW frigates of the 14th Squadron--INS Khukri and the INS Kirpan--were dispatched to deal with the submarine.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
